## 1. Central Management & Networking Software

* 
* vCenter Server: The centralized management engine. It aggregates multiple physical ESXi hosts into a single pane of glass to deploy virtual machines, assign permissions, and orchestrate complex workloads. [4, 5, 6] 
* vSphere Client: The modern, HTML5-based web console administrators log into to configure settings, pull alerts, and monitor hardware performance. [4] 
* vSphere Distributed Switch (vDS): An enterprise virtual switch that crosses all ESXi hosts in a cluster. It allows network configurations, VLAN tags, and traffic-shaping rules to be managed once at the vCenter level instead of individually on every host. [7] 
* vSphere Cluster Services (vCLS): A mandatory backend architecture introduced to decouple automation from vCenter. It deploys a set of small, self-managed agent virtual machines directly inside clusters to keep DRS and HA functioning perfectly even if the main vCenter server goes completely offline. [8] 
