## 2. Software-Defined Storage (vSAN)
[VMware vSAN](https://www.vmware.com/products/cloud-infrastructure/vsan) is a software-defined storage layer built natively into the ESXi kernel. It aggregates local SSDs and NVMe drives from multiple physical hosts into a single, high-performance shared data repository. [9, 10, 11] 

* 
* vSAN Express Storage Architecture (ESA): The advanced storage engine optimized for high-performance NVMe hardware. It leverages a log-structured file system to handle snapshots, global deduplication, and data compression with near-zero performance overhead.
* vSAN Original Storage Architecture (OSA): The traditional disk pool model that divides local drives into a "Caching Tier" and a separate "Capacity Tier".
* Storage Policy-Based Management (SPBM): An automated framework that lets you define storage profiles (e.g., "RAID-1 Mirroring" or "RAID-6 Erasure Coding"). You apply these to specific VMs, and vSAN dynamically handles block placement to ensure policy compliance.
* vSAN Witness Host Appliance: A lightweight, specialized ESXi virtual appliance used in 2-host or stretched (multi-site) clusters. It stores metadata blocks to act as a tie-breaker if network splits cause communication failures. [1, 10, 12, 13, 14] 
