## 3. Core Automation & Availability Features
These features turn individual servers into highly resilient clusters:

| Feature | Primary Function | How It Works |
|---|---|---|
| vSphere vMotion | Non-disruptive VM movement | Migrates a live, running virtual machine from one physical host to another over the network with zero user downtime. |
| Storage vMotion | Non-disruptive disk movement | Migrates a running VM's virtual disk files from one physical storage array to another with no operational interruption. |
| vSphere DRS (Distributed Resource Scheduler) | Automated load balancing | Constantly tracks host CPU and memory use. If a host becomes heavily overloaded, DRS uses live vMotion to cleanly shift VMs to underutilized hardware. |
| vSphere HA (High Availability) | Host crash survival | Constantly monitors physical host heartbeats. If a host completely dies, vSphere HA automatically reboots those exact virtual machines on surviving hardware. |
| vSphere Fault Tolerance (FT) | Zero-downtime protection | Spawns an identical, secondary lock-step copy of a critical VM on a separate host. If the primary host goes black, the shadow VM instantly steps in with zero packet loss or delay. |
| vSphere Storage DRS | Storage optimization | Aggregates individual datastores into storage clusters. It balances input/output (I/O) bottlenecks and capacity strain by automatically moving virtual disks across physical storage systems. |

